WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.002
Almost eight years ago,

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.504
Baiju Bhatt and I founded Robinhood.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:07.048
We believed then as we do now

00:00:07.048 --> 00:00:08.967
that the financial system should be built

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:12.178
to work for everyone,
not just a select few.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.096
It's really simple,

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:14.305
just press three buttons

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:16.558
and then you already got the
amount of crypto that you want.

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:17.726
Just about any stock I've wanted,

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:19.227
I've been able to grab

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:20.895
and it's been pretty easy to use

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:22.105
and I've felt pretty safe.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:24.774
Robinhood is a stock trading application

00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:28.111
that's really popular among
the younger investing crowd.

00:00:28.111 --> 00:00:31.114
So, mainly Gen Zers and Millennials

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.825
and one of the reasons why
it is so popular among them

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.368
is because of its design.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.580
Just from a design angle,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.290
if you open Robinhood's app

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.585
and you open the other apps for Schwab

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.171
or TD Ameritrade or E*TRADE,

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.548
you can tell that they look different.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.216
Trying to convince investors

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.219
that trading is simple and easy

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.472
is not new to Robinhood.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.515
Robinhood's just been more successful

00:00:57.515 --> 00:01:00.143
at making it simple and easy.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.812
Robinhood's design
enables users to sign up

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:06.107
and start trading within
a matter of minutes.

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:08.068
Its colorful, easy-to-use interface

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:11.196
resembles something more
at home in Silicon Valley

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:13.031
than Wall Street.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:14.866
Robinhood's mission has always been

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:16.451
to democratize finance.

00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:19.287
And so, when they set
out to build their app,

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:22.791
what they wanted to do was to
remove any barriers of entry

00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:24.459
that have existed for people

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.087
who've wanted to invest
in the stock market,

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.339
but haven't been able to before.

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:31.007
Imagine taking $100

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:32.884
and turning that into $1,000.

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:34.719
I see a stock going up and I buy it

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:36.930
and I just watch it
until it stops going up

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:37.847
and then I sell it.

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:40.266
Let's see this one keep on falling, 1.57,

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.810
let's keep it going on down, baby.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.894
We're cracking trend lines

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.479
and let's just lock it on up, baby.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:45.897
Let's just lock it on up

00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:48.441
and take $50,000 in a day!

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:49.275
Let's go!

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:52.070
It was really a wake up call

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.532
to a new kind of investor

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:56.991
and a kind of investor

00:01:56.991 --> 00:01:59.953
that, in many cases, was using
Robinhood to place trades

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.790
and trade, not based on
fundamentals necessarily,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.043
but based on something
that seemed like fun.

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:08.920
While Robinhood isn't the only app

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.588
that's design and usability

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:13.049
blurs the lines between investing,

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:15.218
entertainment and gambling,

00:02:15.218 --> 00:02:17.178
it's left many people asking,

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:20.557
is this product a little
too good at what it does?

00:02:21.891 --> 00:02:23.184
There's something to learn

00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:24.978
between the old world and the new world.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.397
The new world is much more frictionless,

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:29.399
giving a lot more access to everyone else.

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:31.276
So, I think that change is happening

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.279
and a lot of the old world applications

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:38.658
are still not understanding
what it takes, to be honest.

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:42.203
So, we're just in a
completely uncharted territory

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:43.496
in some ways.

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.498
This is a new type of behavior

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.375
that's emerged in the past year.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:51.045
Like, meme stock was not
in the vernacular in 2020,

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:51.963
is all I'm saying.

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:04.809
During the pandemic through 2020,

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:08.938
we saw that app usage grew on average 40%.

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:11.900
There were about 35 billion downloads.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:12.942
What we found is that,

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:16.237
whilst users have a lot
of apps on their phone.

00:03:16.237 --> 00:03:19.032
So, typically 80 or so
apps on their phone.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:23.328
Less than half of them are
used on a monthly basis.

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:25.788
And so now, it's not just
about creating an app,

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:30.043
it's about making sure that
you get a user to download it,

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.003
making sure that the app's sticky enough

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:34.589
that they come back on
your daily active users

00:03:34.589 --> 00:03:36.090
or your weekly active users.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.884
Those numbers are also high.

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:42.305
The pandemic offered an opportunity

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:45.808
for older apps that had
plateaued in user growth.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.436
As people delved deeper
into the app store,

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:50.521
the products that proved most appealing

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:51.481
were the ones that offered

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:54.400
something more long
lasting to their users,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.904
be it a language, a new skill or money.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.615
So, as soon as the
coronavirus pandemic hit,

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:02.617
what happened was a lot of people

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:05.036
started spending a lot of time at home

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:07.038
and that boredom really led to them

00:04:07.038 --> 00:04:10.250
trying to find out new things to do

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.586
and the interest in
investing really peaked

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:14.796
in the middle of the pandemic

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:16.339
because people were at home

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.882
and they had access to their phones.

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:19.842
And for the first time,

00:04:19.842 --> 00:04:23.638
Robinhood became an app
of massive interest.

00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:26.099
It's been around since early 2010s,

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:28.893
but it really didn't go off the way it did

00:04:28.893 --> 00:04:30.478
until the pandemic hit.

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:34.524
There's no question that Robinhood

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:38.111
has been wildly popular
in attracting users.

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:41.072
You've got more than
22 million active users

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:42.573
on the platform now

00:04:42.573 --> 00:04:44.617
and that's just in a
matter of a couple of years

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:45.743
that they've built that base.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:49.080
The platform opened up
to investors in 2015.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.668
So, the growth that they've
seen has been astronomical.

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:55.128
So, I think what you need to understand

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.170
when you're trying to understand

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:59.382
why the Robinhood user
experience is that good

00:04:59.382 --> 00:05:00.550
is who built it.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.845
And we're talking about the
co-founders of Robinhood here,

00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:05.638
Vlad Tenev and Baiju Bhatt.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.891
These were two guys who met at Stanford

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:12.937
at a time when Facebook,
Twitter, Instagram,

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:15.315
all of those apps were really taking off

00:05:15.315 --> 00:05:17.483
and had really established what it means

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:21.154
to be an application that
drives user engagement

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:23.114
and really draws people in.

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.575
Think about pop-up notifications

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.660
or the ability to engage
with your friends.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.621
So, if you invite a
friend to the platform,

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:32.332
you get a free stock.

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:34.625
All of those things hadn't
really existed before

00:05:34.625 --> 00:05:36.753
and so, they really pioneered the way

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:39.797
for investing to become
way more democratized

00:05:39.797 --> 00:05:43.551
through a very social
media like experience.

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:46.220
So, I'm opening my Robinhood app

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.639
and I see a nice little green screen

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:50.641
with the feather logo in the back

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:54.187
and what comes up is this
very clean interface.

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:57.607
You don't have to type anything
into your phone, right?

00:05:57.607 --> 00:05:59.942
So, you get lists of things

00:05:59.942 --> 00:06:01.486
that you can just scroll up or down

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:03.196
to click the correct answer.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:04.739
And I know it's really trivial, right?

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:06.866
Like, you can just type in New York

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.827
rather than having to
scroll up and pick New York,

00:06:09.827 --> 00:06:11.788
but those are sort of the design features

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:14.374
that, when you talk to experts, will say,

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:15.750
it's just easier, right?

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.460
You don't have to type anything in.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.129
They would tell you that
that is just, again,

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:21.798
sort of bringing down barriers

00:06:21.798 --> 00:06:23.216
and making it easier for people,

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:25.093
from a design point of view,

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:26.594
to sort of sign up.

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:30.056
A critical element for Robinhood

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.809
is making that first trade for these users

00:06:32.809 --> 00:06:34.769
as easy as possible.

00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:37.146
When signing up to most brokerages,

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.856
a user will have to wait a few days

00:06:38.856 --> 00:06:41.067
before money is deposited
into their account

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.152
and they can start trading.

00:06:43.152 --> 00:06:44.404
But Robinhood lets users

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:46.948
trade up to a thousand dollars instantly

00:06:46.948 --> 00:06:48.991
while waiting for their account to clear.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.036
If I were a first-time investor,

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:55.164
I would not find anything
intimidating about this.

00:06:55.164 --> 00:06:58.292
It's kind of welcoming, honestly.

00:06:58.292 --> 00:06:59.627
Right up front,

00:06:59.627 --> 00:07:02.755
there's a big button to click trade,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.465
I can see the volume

00:07:04.465 --> 00:07:05.591
and I have the option

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:09.720
to either buy shares of, let's say, Apple

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.305
or trade options on it if I want,

00:07:11.305 --> 00:07:12.682
that's right there for me too.

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:15.560
And so, really what you
see in the Robinhood app

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.312
is just very little information

00:07:18.312 --> 00:07:21.441
presented in a very user-friendly way

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:24.318
with a lot of colors,
with a lot of animation,

00:07:24.318 --> 00:07:26.779
and that's something that
hadn't really existed

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.532
in brokerage platforms before.

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:32.452
From data font sizes
that help direct users

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.287
to what they should focus on

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:38.207
to the snowflake animation
that falls on December 25th,

00:07:38.207 --> 00:07:40.042
Robinhood has been very deliberate

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:42.462
in all its design choices.

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:45.465
Take the cryptocurrency
trading page for example,

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:47.592
its interface reflects the excitement

00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:50.136
and buzz around this type of investing.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:53.931
It almost looks like
you're in like a video game

00:07:53.931 --> 00:07:57.018
or an ad for like an
energy drink or something.

00:07:57.018 --> 00:07:59.812
It's a design element
that's completely different

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:02.064
from anything that I've seen

00:08:02.064 --> 00:08:04.484
on a traditional trading platform.

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:09.447
There's a lot of these
delightful graphical moments

00:08:09.447 --> 00:08:12.533
where it's just reflective

00:08:12.533 --> 00:08:15.786
of almost an artist signing their work.

00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:16.954
You've gone from 15 years ago,

00:08:16.954 --> 00:08:18.372
people seeing folks sitting

00:08:18.372 --> 00:08:21.125
with six or seven screens and trading

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:24.504
to realizing actually
you've got your mobile phone

00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:25.796
and a small screen

00:08:25.796 --> 00:08:28.633
and you can achieve
the same outcome really

00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:29.592
as an individual.

00:08:29.592 --> 00:08:31.344
Those trading systems, the data,

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:33.304
the exchanges, the trading exchanges,

00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:35.431
they've been the same for years and years.

00:08:35.431 --> 00:08:37.767
What's changed is there
is now a user interface

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:41.270
that makes it easily accessible
to a broader set of users.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:42.104
We're proud of the fact

00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:45.316
that we've enabled so
many younger investors

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:48.152
and first time investors to
have access to the markets.

00:08:48.152 --> 00:08:49.737
I think design is at the heart of it

00:08:49.737 --> 00:08:51.781
because the ease of use,

00:08:51.781 --> 00:08:54.575
the enjoyment that a user
gets using the application

00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:55.409
is so important.

00:08:56.452 --> 00:08:59.956
Over half of Robinhood uses
are first-time investors,

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:03.501
with 70% of its assets held
by millennial investors

00:09:03.501 --> 00:09:05.920
between 18 and 40.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.215
It's popularity among young
investors has been in part

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:12.510
down to a frictionless user experience.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.887
Friction refers to something in the design

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:17.515
that slows you down in
accomplishing your goal.

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:18.599
This may sound familiar

00:09:18.599 --> 00:09:22.019
if you've ever tried to
unsubscribe from a mailing list

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:25.022
and being presented with
something like this.

00:09:25.022 --> 00:09:26.774
One of the things that
people talk a lot about

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:28.568
is frictionless user experience.

00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:30.403
Designers, more and more, are realizing

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:32.363
that users are unforgiving.

00:09:32.363 --> 00:09:35.783
They will drop off during the registration

00:09:35.783 --> 00:09:36.617
or sign-up process.

00:09:36.617 --> 00:09:39.579
They will drop off if
they get stuck on a screen

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:42.790
and can't work out what
the next call to action is.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.043
Designers are now starting
to value user feedback,

00:09:46.043 --> 00:09:49.255
look at what users are doing
in terms of using an app.

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.465
So, tracking through heat maps, etc,

00:09:51.465 --> 00:09:53.467
what kind of utilization the app has

00:09:53.467 --> 00:09:55.886
from a user perspective.

00:09:55.886 --> 00:09:57.263
If you look back at how Robinhood

00:09:57.263 --> 00:09:59.557
really sort of created
this user interface,

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:03.853
was by going out to coffee stands

00:10:03.853 --> 00:10:05.896
and coffee shops at Stanford

00:10:05.896 --> 00:10:08.608
and going up to students and asking them

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:10.776
how they felt about investing

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:12.945
and how they felt about the
apps that were out there

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:15.406
and really getting the
sort of feedback from users

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:17.199
about what they wanted to see

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:18.576
and what they didn't want to see.

00:10:18.576 --> 00:10:20.119
And even up until this day,

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:21.871
Robinhood research team

00:10:21.871 --> 00:10:24.123
is really, really prevalent in the company

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:25.583
and they really go out of their way

00:10:25.583 --> 00:10:29.378
to sort of try to give
users what they want,

00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:32.173
but there's cost to that.

00:10:32.173 --> 00:10:34.091
While frictionless user experience

00:10:34.091 --> 00:10:36.218
may be what customers want,

00:10:36.218 --> 00:10:39.055
some argue the frictions
in investment apps

00:10:39.055 --> 00:10:41.349
can actually work as a safety mechanism.

00:10:42.933 --> 00:10:45.895
The problem for many investors

00:10:45.895 --> 00:10:49.106
with making trading too easy

00:10:49.106 --> 00:10:53.819
is that oftentimes frictions
take the place of self-control.

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.030
And when something is too easy,

00:10:56.030 --> 00:11:00.785
we may do it more than is
in our own best interests.

00:11:00.785 --> 00:11:04.038
So, I've done research
over the last few decades

00:11:04.038 --> 00:11:06.207
that showed that, on average,

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:09.543
when individual investors
trades more actively,

00:11:09.543 --> 00:11:12.338
they end up worse off.

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:15.800
There's not a great deal
of information on the app

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.219
compared to other brokerage sites.

00:11:18.219 --> 00:11:21.138
And one thing that does
is gives one the feeling

00:11:21.138 --> 00:11:24.600
that this is not too complicated

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:28.104
of a thing to do, to
buy and sell stocks.

00:11:28.104 --> 00:11:31.065
And clearly, the mechanics of
buying and selling are easy,

00:11:31.065 --> 00:11:33.859
but choosing stocks,

00:11:35.319 --> 00:11:37.488
analyzing stocks can be difficult.

00:11:37.488 --> 00:11:39.490
We put a great value in this country,

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:43.285
in letting people have the
freedom to do what they want

00:11:44.328 --> 00:11:47.748
and yet, we also want to
have some protections.

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:51.293
The family of 20 year-old Alex Kearns

00:11:51.293 --> 00:11:53.921
is suing popular stock
trading app, Robinhood,

00:11:53.921 --> 00:11:57.091
after Kearns died by suicide last year,

00:11:57.091 --> 00:12:00.386
believing he was hundreds of
thousands of dollars in debt.

00:12:00.386 --> 00:12:02.430
The worst aspect of what they do, Chloe,

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:06.892
is the way they're gamifying
the idea of investing.

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:08.978
They're taking unsophisticated investors,

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:10.938
most of whom have no experience,

00:12:10.938 --> 00:12:13.482
and basically making this into a game,

00:12:13.482 --> 00:12:15.276
causing them to suffer losses.

00:12:15.276 --> 00:12:18.863
Robinhood has faced some
pretty serious criticism

00:12:18.863 --> 00:12:23.242
that it's making investing almost too fun,

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:26.370
which seems like a strange criticism.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.458
But the point is that
they've made investing

00:12:30.458 --> 00:12:32.960
so accessible and so easy

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.338
that they might be pushing traders

00:12:36.338 --> 00:12:38.883
to take risks that they don't understand

00:12:38.883 --> 00:12:41.135
or outsize risks in financial markets

00:12:41.135 --> 00:12:43.721
or see stock trading as kind of a game

00:12:43.721 --> 00:12:45.806
or kind of a gamble.

00:12:45.806 --> 00:12:47.600
And for the critics of Robinhood,

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.018
there's one design element

00:12:49.018 --> 00:12:51.645
that never fails to get mentioned.

00:12:51.645 --> 00:12:54.273
I'm a bit surprised by how much attention

00:12:54.273 --> 00:12:55.941
the confetti animation has gotten.

00:12:55.941 --> 00:12:58.819
It's literally an animation that comes up

00:12:58.819 --> 00:13:00.863
after you make your first trade.

00:13:00.863 --> 00:13:05.159
You think, from reading
some of the commentary,

00:13:05.159 --> 00:13:09.872
that confetti's flying through
the app all over the place.

00:13:09.872 --> 00:13:11.499
The story behind the confetti

00:13:11.499 --> 00:13:15.377
is that Robinhood used to
display a confetti graphic

00:13:15.377 --> 00:13:18.672
to celebrate the first trade
that you made on the platform.

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:21.175
And the confetti motif also came up

00:13:21.175 --> 00:13:23.427
in its ad campaigns early on,

00:13:23.427 --> 00:13:26.555
where you would see people
trading on their Robinhood app.

00:13:28.015 --> 00:13:30.810
With an app that's changing
the way we do money.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.812
Now, what came to pass

00:13:32.812 --> 00:13:36.607
was confetti became like the
thing people would point to

00:13:36.607 --> 00:13:40.069
when they said Robinhood
is gamifying investing.

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:41.904
Eventually, Robinhood scrapped

00:13:41.904 --> 00:13:43.739
the confetti graphic altogether,

00:13:43.739 --> 00:13:44.949
but it's not like you're going to see

00:13:44.949 --> 00:13:46.492
people migrate away from Robinhood

00:13:46.492 --> 00:13:48.452
just because they don't
show confetti anymore

00:13:48.452 --> 00:13:50.830
when you place your first trade.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.457
In my mind, gamification is so broad.

00:13:53.457 --> 00:13:55.209
It's anything that allows you

00:13:55.209 --> 00:13:59.088
to almost experiment
and enjoy the process.

00:13:59.088 --> 00:14:01.507
The fear that a lot of regulators have

00:14:01.507 --> 00:14:05.469
is that retail investors
don't have the knowledge base

00:14:05.469 --> 00:14:07.388
that professional investors have

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:10.015
and as a result of
things like gamification

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:11.225
or stickiness in an app,

00:14:11.225 --> 00:14:13.727
they'll almost be brainwashed into

00:14:13.727 --> 00:14:16.355
or influenced into making
investments or taking risks

00:14:16.355 --> 00:14:19.483
that they otherwise would
not be comfortable doing.

00:14:19.483 --> 00:14:21.026
But I actually don't
think that's the case.

00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:23.404
I think users are going
on to apps, etc,

00:14:23.404 --> 00:14:25.239
because they want to invest.

00:14:25.239 --> 00:14:29.451
They want to try investing
in different securities

00:14:29.451 --> 00:14:32.204
that otherwise they didn't have access to.

00:14:32.204 --> 00:14:33.747
And there was a particular stock

00:14:33.747 --> 00:14:37.418
that really captured the
imagination of these new investors.

00:14:37.418 --> 00:14:38.544
In January this year,

00:14:38.544 --> 00:14:41.088
users of Robinhood and other trading apps

00:14:41.088 --> 00:14:43.716
pushed up the price of GameStop options,

00:14:43.716 --> 00:14:44.800
leaving hedge funds

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.928
facing hundreds of millions
of dollars in losses.

00:14:47.928 --> 00:14:50.139
Let's start off with
what is almost bordering

00:14:50.139 --> 00:14:51.682
on the ridiculousness here

00:14:51.682 --> 00:14:53.475
with regards to the GameStop trade.

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:56.020
Do you see a fundamental reordering here

00:14:56.020 --> 00:15:00.107
in the hierarchy of who has
the power on Wall Street?

00:15:00.107 --> 00:15:01.901
A platform like Robinhood

00:15:01.901 --> 00:15:04.320
was the perfect sort of vehicle

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.281
to create this frenzy in trading

00:15:07.281 --> 00:15:09.450
because it made it so easy

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:14.538
that now so many people
had access to this stock

00:15:14.538 --> 00:15:16.624
and it took off.

00:15:16.624 --> 00:15:18.375
Of course, what we've
all been talking about,

00:15:18.375 --> 00:15:20.127
a stock which has now
been on a rocket ship

00:15:20.127 --> 00:15:21.587
as part of this short squeeze

00:15:21.587 --> 00:15:23.881
and whether those that
are investing in this

00:15:23.881 --> 00:15:26.842
fully understand what
they're investing in.

00:15:26.842 --> 00:15:29.261
Robinhood customers
with all of their traits

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:31.138
like younger,

00:15:31.138 --> 00:15:35.017
maybe more plugged in to
online message boards,

00:15:35.017 --> 00:15:37.311
maybe with a little bit
less than their account

00:15:37.311 --> 00:15:41.690
and more of an appetite
for YOLO-type investing,

00:15:41.690 --> 00:15:46.320
this all came to a head
in the GameStop saga

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:49.323
and it was the first time
that we'd really seen

00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:53.452
such a mass coordination
of retail investors online.

00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:54.912
In the wake of GameStop,

00:15:54.912 --> 00:15:56.914
Robinhood's CEO, Vlad Tenev,

00:15:56.914 --> 00:15:58.666
testified before Congress.

00:15:58.666 --> 00:16:00.918
He was questioned on a variety of topics,

00:16:00.918 --> 00:16:04.964
including his decision to halt
customers buying these stocks

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:07.675
and faced scrutiny over the app's design.

00:16:07.675 --> 00:16:10.302
Confetti falls every
time they place an order,

00:16:10.302 --> 00:16:13.931
they get push notifications,
they're encouraged to trade.

00:16:13.931 --> 00:16:17.226
If a friend signs up, they
get a free stock, on and on.

00:16:17.226 --> 00:16:20.562
Why have you added specific
gaming design elements

00:16:20.562 --> 00:16:22.940
to look like gambling to your app?

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.108
There was also criticism

00:16:24.108 --> 00:16:26.527
of the way Robinhood makes its money.

00:16:26.527 --> 00:16:28.904
Instead of taking a slice from the user,

00:16:28.904 --> 00:16:30.239
Robinhood takes its cut

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:33.158
by selling customers
orders to trading firms.

00:16:33.158 --> 00:16:35.786
Put simply, the more a user trades,

00:16:35.786 --> 00:16:37.621
the more money Robinhood makes,

00:16:37.621 --> 00:16:38.747
leaving some to question

00:16:38.747 --> 00:16:41.917
whether the apps frictionless
user-friendly design

00:16:41.917 --> 00:16:44.670
was actually in its user's best interest.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:49.675
In 2020, Robinhood earned
about 75% of its revenue

00:16:49.675 --> 00:16:52.720
based on transactions
happening on the platform.

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:55.639
So, that gives its
critics ammunition to say

00:16:55.639 --> 00:16:57.558
Robinhood wants you to trade more,

00:16:57.558 --> 00:17:00.644
whether or not it's good
for your financial health

00:17:00.644 --> 00:17:04.606
to be buying and selling
stocks all day or all week.

00:17:04.606 --> 00:17:09.570
Robinhood is there making
money from transactions

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:13.157
and at present, without
that source of revenue,

00:17:13.157 --> 00:17:13.991
I mean,

00:17:13.991 --> 00:17:19.329
it would lose three quarters
of its business, basically.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.665
Now, the U.S. Security
and Exchange Commission

00:17:21.665 --> 00:17:24.710
is weighing whether to ban
this way of doing business.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.170
Robinhood's legal chief,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:29.131
who himself is a former SEC commissioner,

00:17:29.131 --> 00:17:32.718
said banning payment for
order flow would be "draconian".

00:17:32.718 --> 00:17:34.595
What you're going to see moving forward

00:17:34.595 --> 00:17:37.222
is a lot more regulatory scrutiny

00:17:37.222 --> 00:17:38.891
and a lot more regulation

00:17:38.891 --> 00:17:43.854
to set in place some
protections for these investors.

00:17:43.854 --> 00:17:46.315
But I don't think it's
going to happen quickly.

00:17:46.315 --> 00:17:48.067
I think it's going to take time

00:17:48.067 --> 00:17:49.485
and in the meantime,

00:17:49.485 --> 00:17:52.696
having access to these financial tools

00:17:52.696 --> 00:17:55.199
is going to go one of two ways.

00:17:55.199 --> 00:17:57.034
It's either going to help people

00:17:57.034 --> 00:17:58.827
learn more about the stock market

00:17:58.827 --> 00:18:02.456
or it's going to help
them lose a lot of money.

00:18:02.456 --> 00:18:04.374
I think it would be completely crazy

00:18:05.417 --> 00:18:08.462
to almost penalize someone

00:18:08.462 --> 00:18:11.215
for building a high quality,

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:14.134
high functioning well-designed app.

00:18:14.134 --> 00:18:17.930
Innovation is inherently
fraught with risk.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:22.184
Of course, the utility they
offer can be regulated,

00:18:22.184 --> 00:18:25.896
but the fact that they make
it easy for users to use

00:18:25.896 --> 00:18:27.940
and make it fun for users to use

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:31.652
has to fall outside that
regulatory set of restrictions.

00:18:31.652 --> 00:18:33.737
I think that's something that,

00:18:33.737 --> 00:18:35.072
if you start regulating that,

00:18:35.072 --> 00:18:36.490
you're stifling innovation.

